</code></pre>

Q: Can this palindrome-with-one-deletion algorithm be improved beyond my current approach? A: <p>This is a LeetCode problem (<a href="https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-palindrome-ii/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">link</a>). The worst-case time complexity of your solution is already Θ(n), which is optimal. It is not Θ(n²) because <code>count</code> is at most 2.</p>
<p>Here is a simplified version:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>class Solution:
    def validPalindrome(self, s: str) -&gt; bool:
        i, j = 0, len(s) - 1
        while i &lt; j and s[i] == s[j]:
            i, j = i + 1, j - 1
        t = s[i : j + 1]
        return t[1:] == t[1:][::-1] or t[:-1] == t[:-1][::-1]
</code></pre>
<p>Note that both your solution and the code above require O(n) space, because <code>str</code> slicing in Python creates copies.</p>
<p>Here is a pure two-pointer version that uses O(1) extra space:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>class Solution:
    def validPalindrome(self, s: str) -&gt; bool:
        def check(i: int, j: int) -&gt; bool:
            while i &lt; j:
                if s[i] != s[j]:
                    return False
                i, j = i + 1, j - 1
            return True

        i, j = 0, len(s) - 1
        while i &lt; j and s[i] == s[j]:
            i, j = i + 1, j - 1
        return check(i + 1, j) or check(i, j - 1)
</code></pre>
<p>Despite using less space, this version may run slower in CPython because its comparisons are performed in Python loops, whereas slicing and string comparison are implemented in optimized C code.</p>

